To make the file executable (runnable):
      chmod u+x file_name.sh                          #chmod = changes file user permissions to executable mode for the file
Review of chmod symbolic permissions:
      = means set the permission exactly
      + = add
      - = remove 
    
      u = user
      g = group
      o = other
      a = all  types

      r = read (view file contents)
      w = write (allows modify, overwrite, or delete)
      x = execute (run file as script)
Review of chmod numeric permissions:
      4 = read
      2 = write
      1 = execute

      Example: chmod 756 file_name.sh
      7 = users has read, write, execute (4+2+1 = 7)
      5 = group has read and execute (4+1)
      6 = others have read and write (4+2)
Chmod for folders
      r (4) = read
      w (2) = write
      x (1) = travel through directory
Chmod folder option
      chmod -R 755 my_folder = means recursive, changes folder & all files in folde permissions to rwx-rx-rx
Chmod file option
      chmod -referenece=old_file.txt new_file.txt = copy old file permissions to new file
Review different ways to run files
      ./file_name.sh = tells computer to run file located in current directory (must give file executable permission or will be denied) 
      bash file_name.sh (prepended with bash means it will run using bash)
      sh file_name.sh (prepended with sh for shell means it will use the shell terminal's program to run file - could be bash or something else)
      Practice Breaking Stuff:
          Removed executable permissions from files, all groups only contained read or write permissions for test files
              Result:
                      ./ method   =  permission denied
                      bash method =  file runs without x permission
                      sh method   =  file runs without x permission
              Conclusion:
                      ./ method is explicit method, must change mode to x permission to run as script
                      bash doesn't need mode changed to x permission to run as script
                      sh doesn't need mode changed to x permission to run as script
Variables in Bash
      No data types
      Use "=" to assign values directly to variable
            small example: country=united_states
      Use "$" to access variable from output or another variable
            small example: second_var=$country          
